  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Birago Diop was Senegalese, a veterinarian, a diplomat and a writer of the Négritude generation.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Négritude was the Francophone African and Caribbean movement that sought to revalue black culture and the African past; Diop worked alongside Senghor.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Six stanzas of uneven length, with no fixed metre and no rhyme, held together by repetition and by rhetorical question.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Six stanzas: the telling, the crying, the question of the heart, the coming of the dead, the signs they left, and the verdict.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The collective voice is the poem's method: the speaker includes himself in the failure, which is why the poem accuses and mourns at once.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The opening clause is conditional: if we tell gently, all we have to tell, who will hear us without laughter? The expected answer is no one.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A beggar is heard only if someone chooses to give; the image makes the people's appeal humiliating as well as urgent.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The mouth is open but the sound is ridiculous, and the powerful are figured as children who laugh at it.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The anger grows inside the people, in the throat, so that the illness is both emotional and connected to speech.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The throat is the organ of speech, which links the anger to the poem's larger subject: the failure to be heard.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "When our Dead come with their Dead" — the dead bring their own dead, which makes the ancestral world a community extending backwards.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The clumsiness matters: the dead are not majestic, and their awkward speech makes the living's neglect worse, not easier.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "They have left on the earth their cries, / In the air, on the water, where they have traced their signs" — the elements are a record.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The world itself is the archive; the living are illiterate in their own landscape.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The three adjectives name three failures — sight, hearing and worth — and "Sons" makes the failure filial.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "And since we did not understand our dead" — the grammar moves from possibility to settled fact.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "If" leaves the outcome open; "since" treats the failure as already committed, and the poem's question therefore has no remedy.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "What ear to our sobbing hearts?" — the unanswered question is the poem's point, and the reader's silence is the reply.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The older sense — emptiness, futility — is the one the poem uses: the weeping comes too late to change anything.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The poem's verdict is retrospective: since the people did not understand, their mourning cannot be heard as repentance.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The dead are owed remembrance and have been neglected; the speaker's reverence is inseparable from the community's guilt.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The questions repeat with variation, and their number is the argument: a poem that asks nine questions and answers none is demonstrating that no answer is available.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The same gesture returns after the failure, which is what makes it poignant: the gentleness was always there, and it was never enough.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Repetition does the work that rhyme does in a formal lyric: it binds the poem and marks its stages.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The cries are in the air, on the water and on the earth; the landscape is a witness the living cannot read.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The poem names no colonial official and no historical event; it speaks of the dead, the living and the failure of hearing.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The mouths are large and open, shaped by children's laughter, so the people are exposed rather than fed.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    If the dead had been majestic, neglect would be understandable; their clumsiness removes the excuse.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A plaint is a complaint and a lament, and the throat is the organ of both speech and song — the poem keeps its two subjects together.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The "we" includes the speaker, so the accusation falls on the community, and the lament is for what the community has lost.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The dead are owed remembrance, and the failure is a failure of cultural memory — which is why the poem is central to the Négritude argument.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There is no named addressee; the absence of a listener is the poem's subject, and the reader is left in the position of the one who does not answer.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    He trained as a veterinary surgeon in France and later served as Senegal's ambassador; the biography is worth one fact, not a paragraph.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Vanité carries the same double sense of emptiness and futility, which the English Vanity preserves.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The move from "if we tell" to "and since we did not understand" is what turns a lament into a judgment, and questions on structure and tone depend on it.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    They come, they speak, they leave signs; the neglect is the living's, not a sign that the dead are unreal.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It grows in them, in the throat: not a weapon turned outward but an illness turned inward.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The relation is the mark; the two poems share a subject but distribute the blame differently, and that difference is the answer.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "What ear to our sobbing hearts?" refuses the consolation a formal lyric would offer, which is why the poem ends where it does.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Position questions cannot be answered from a study guide; they need the poem, which is why the stanza table is written from the text.
  ]

]

